\section*{Generalized Hodge Conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Manuscript 1 (family 032, 2026-09-30): On the specified quadratic-lattice locus of projective K3 surfaces, all rational Hodge classes on every power are claimed to be algebraic; an exact algebraic Kuga--Satake tensor also gives a conditional criterion beyond that locus. Manuscript 2 (family 032, 2026-09-30): The manuscripts claim algebraicity of rational Hodge classes on CM abelian varieties, with consequences for generalized Hodge classes and finite-field Tate classes. Manuscript 3 (family 040, 2026-09-24): For every smooth complex projective surface with $p_g=0$, OpenAI claims that the Albanese map on integral degree-zero cycles is an isomorphism.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For a projective complex K3 surface $S$, write $T_S=\operatorname{NS}(S)_{\mathbb Q}^{\perp}$ with cup-product quadratic form $q$. Assume a rational isometric embedding
\[
(T_S,q)\hookrightarrow\mathbb U_{\mathbb Q}^{\oplus2}\perp\langle-1\rangle^4,
\]
where $\mathbb U$ is the hyperbolic plane with Gram matrix $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. Choose any full even-Clifford realization $H^1(A,\mathbb Q)=C^+(T_S,q)$, any $w\in T_S$ with $q(w)\ne0$, and any rational weight-one polarization. The Kuga--Satake map is formed from $v\mapsto(a\mapsto vaw)$ and this polarization. OpenAI asserts existence of $\Gamma\in\operatorname{CH}^2(S\times A^2)_{\mathbb Q}$ inducing this exact prescribed map on $T_S$, also after transporting it to an isogenous realization. It further claims rational Hodge and generalized Hodge conjectures in all degrees of $S^m$ for every $m\ge1$. The quadratic embedding is a hypothesis, so this theorem alone is restricted to that locus.

\paragraph{Manuscript 2.}
A complex abelian variety $A$ is CM if $\operatorname{End}(A)\otimes\mathbb Q$ contains a commutative semisimple algebra of dimension $2\dim A$. For every such $A$ and every $p\ge0$, OpenAI claims
\[
\operatorname{CH}^p(A)_{\mathbb Q}\longrightarrow H^{2p}(A,\mathbb Q)\cap H^{p,p}(A)
\quad\text{is surjective}.
\]
This also applies to arbitrary finite products of CM abelian varieties, including their powers. If $W\subset H^k(A,\mathbb Q)$ is a rational Hodge substructure and $W(r)$ is effective (all Hodge bidegrees are nonnegative), it claims a closed algebraic $Z\subset A$ of codimension at least $r$ with $W\subset\ker[H^k(A,\mathbb Q)\to H^k(A\setminus Z,\mathbb Q)]$.
The manuscript additionally asserts that, for every abelian variety $A/\mathbb F_q$, every $r\ge0$ and prime $\ell\ne\operatorname{char}\mathbb F_q$,
\[
\operatorname{CH}^r(A)\otimes\mathbb Q_\ell\longrightarrow
H^{2r}_{\mathrm{\acute et}}(A_{\overline{\mathbb F}_q},\mathbb Q_\ell(r))^{\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb F}_q/\mathbb F_q)}
\quad\text{is surjective}.
\]
Numerical and $\ell$-adic homological equivalence are claimed to agree on rational cycles. Over any algebraically closed field, for an abelian $g$-fold and rational ample class $h$, the form $(-1)^r\deg(xyh^{g-2r})$ is claimed positive definite on the real extension of $\ker[h^{g-2r+1}:A^r_{\mathrm{num}}\to A^{g-r+1}_{\mathrm{num}}]$ for $0\le r\le g/2$.

\paragraph{Manuscript 3.}
For a smooth connected projective complex surface $S$ put $p_g=h^0(S,\omega_S)$ and $q=h^1(S,\mathcal O_S)$. OpenAI asserts the integral statements
\[
p_g=q=0\ \Longrightarrow\ \deg:\operatorname{CH}_0(S)\xrightarrow{\sim}\mathbb Z,
\qquad
p_g=0\ \Longrightarrow\ \operatorname{alb}_S:\operatorname{CH}_0(S)^0\xrightarrow{\sim}\operatorname{Alb}(S)(\mathbb C),
\]
where $\operatorname{CH}_0(S)^0$ is the subgroup of degree-zero zero-cycles modulo rational equivalence. Under $p_g=0$, it also claims that the rational Chow motive is finite-dimensional in Kimura's sense and has zero transcendental degree-two summand. When $p_g=q=0$ it claims
\[
h(S)_{\mathbb Q}\simeq\mathbf1\oplus\mathbb L^{\oplus b_2(S)}\oplus\mathbb L^2,
\]
where $\mathbb L$ is the Lefschetz motive. Under the joint hypothesis $p_g=q=0$, for each power $Y=S^m$ and each Hilbert scheme $Y=S^{[n]}$, the corresponding rational motive is pure Tate and $\operatorname{CH}^i(Y)_{\mathbb Q}\xrightarrow{\sim}H^{2i}(Y,\mathbb Q)$, with $H^{2i+1}(Y,\mathbb Q)=0$, for every $i\ge0$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 032, 2026-09-30): Clifford multiplication carries exterior powers of the transcendental space into degree-two cohomology of an abelian target. Transposed cycles and a polynomial inverse on the finite-dimensional image recover these summands, so divisor algebraicity supplies the required classes without an inverse Lefschetz correspondence.

Manuscript 2 (family 032, 2026-09-30): The argument realizes four-factor balanced tensors using unitary theta constructions. Elementary switches between equal-sum lists, followed by algebraic polarization contractions and descent of coefficients, build arbitrary balanced CM tensors from these elementary cycle classes.

Manuscript 3 (family 040, 2026-09-24): A determinant-constrained Quot obstruction theory and torus localization produce a Chow relation involving the diagonal. A nested-Hilbert recursion leaves two surface factors unintegrated, retaining an actual correspondence identity; its external-product terms annihilate degree-zero cycles, and the diagonal coefficient gives the required vanishing after the standard reductions.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 032, 2026-09-30): The main theorem supplies the coniveau statement for powers of K3 surfaces satisfying the additional quadratic-lattice embedding, rather than every smooth projective variety.

Manuscript 2 (family 032, 2026-09-30): The CM generalized-Hodge corollary establishes supports only for CM abelian varieties; our coniveau equality quantifies over every smooth projective complex variety.

Manuscript 3 (family 040, 2026-09-24): The pure-Tate cycle-class consequence supplies geometric support on the stated powers and Hilbert schemes of p\_g=q=0 surfaces; the universal generalized-Hodge coniveau assertion remains outside this class.

\subsection{Formal verification}
